WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.120
Next with tin and lead
solder, they fuse a

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.250
threaded neck ring to
the bottom edge.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.190
This ring attaches the
bonnet to the helmet's

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.860
breastplate, which
bolts to the collar of

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.270
the diver's dry suit.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.270
Then they solder a base
to the face plate

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.600
opening. Like all the
helmets components, it's

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.360
made of red brass,
which is more durable

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.940
than standard brass due
to its higher copper

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.570
content. Through a
template.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.720
They drill holes into a
banana shaped brass

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:30.770
exhaust tube.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.550
It enables the divers
exhaled air to exit the

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.640
helmet. They solder the
exhaust to the bonnet,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.160
positioning the non
perforated end over a

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.950
hole near the diver's
mouth and the perforated

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.120
end at the rear.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.300
This directs air
bubbles behind the diver

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.250
so as not to obstruct
the view.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.220
Then they solder on the
remaining components,

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.480
including three window
bases.

